WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.279
After more than three decades in the movie
industry, three-time Oscar winner Daniel Day-Lewis

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.720
has decided to hang up his hat.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:07.820
"Mr. Daniel?"

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:09.860
"I'm finished."

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.599
The actor announced that his 2017 film with
writer-director Paul Thomas Anderson, a '50s-set

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.120
dress designer drama titled Phantom Thread,
will be the last installment in his esteemed

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:20.640
filmography.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.540
The statement, given via his spokeswoman,
read,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.740
"He is immensely grateful to all of his collaborators
and audiences over the many years.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.420
This is a private decision and neither he
nor his representatives will make any further

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.420
comment on this subject."

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.680
The notoriously private thespian may not be
willing to elaborate publicly, but he has

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.580
plenty of reasons to leave Hollywood's bright
lights behind.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.340
The toll of being a method actor

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.780
It's no secret that Daniel Day-Lewis takes
a very unique approach to his craft.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.780
Not only is he exceedingly choosy about which
roles he'll accept, but he's also known to

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.440
disappear into them throughout the production
process.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.640
"I follow my curiosity and it takes me into
all kinds of strange places."

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.550
He stayed in a wheelchair and was spoon-fed
by crewmembers to fully experience having

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:11.401
cerebral palsy for his Oscar-winning turn
in My Left Foot; he spent six months in the

00:01:11.401 --> 00:01:16.550
wild to prepare for his role in The Last of
the Mohicans; he isolated himself from the

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:21.570
cast and crew during the production on The
Ballad of Jack and Rose; he tattooed his hands

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.880
and trained for 18 months as a fighter for
The Boxer.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.440
The results speak for themselves, but all
that dramatic heavy lifting takes a toll.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.140
"Part of my job is to be drained."

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.000
"I wore myself out."

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.260
Post-production depression

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.060
Not only is the work itself grueling, but
once he's done with a role, he has to deal

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.360
with the emotional repercussions of letting
it go.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.860
As he told The Telegraph, "There's a terrible
sadness.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.220
The last day of shooting is surreal.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.640
Your mind, your body, your spirit are not
prepared to accept that this experience is

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.600
coming to an end.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.280
You've devoted so much of your time to unleashing,
in an unconscious way, some sort of spiritual

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:01.820
turmoil."

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.180
He also has trouble letting go of his characters
as a result.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.520
"You're not quite sure what to do with yourself
when it's finished."

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.200
"It's very hard to conceive of any kind of
life after it.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.780
Of course there is one waiting."]

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.020
For example, after finishing Gangs of New
York, Day-Lewis admitted to speaking with

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:25.170
his Butcher dialect for months after the film's
completion and felt a sense of alienation

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.540
during the film's debut, telling Rolling Stone,

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.420
"I got nervous before the premiere.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.320
I knew I'd also feel a sense of sadness.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.140
Now I have to accept that the film is complete
and no longer has anything to do with me."

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.800
Secondary career paths

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.700
He was showered with decades of praise for
his work in front of the camera, but Day-Lewis

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.560
ultimately sought a different level of fulfillment
in his side career as a cobbler.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.600
After shooting The Boxer, he started a family
with his wife Rebecca Miller, and apprenticed

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.500
in Italy with master shoemaker Stefano Bemer.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.380
He told Rolling Stone, "It's an antidote to
this other thing I do."

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.580
"Most particularly, perhaps, because you see
this visible evidence, you have this tangible

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.100
thing at the end, and if you f*** up, you
can see it very clearly and do it again.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.400
It's not a matter of opinion.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:13.420
It's either good or it's bad."

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:18.850
Making shoes might seem like a strange passion
for an Oscar winner, but as we've seen countless

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:22.640
times, it can be hard for a celebrity to stay
grounded in the spotlight.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.310
Working with one's hands to produce work you
can point to at the end of the day seems like

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.800
a great way to stay centered.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:29.400
Allergic to fame

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:34.480
Even at the peak of his career, Day-Lewis
retreated from the public eye for years between

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:38.900
projects to spend time on his family farm
in Ireland, where he's often spotted visiting

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.760
the pubs to have a pint—alone.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:45.720
Unlike a number of his more outgoing colleagues,
he's rejected the notion that he's a public

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:46.740
figure at all times.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.060
He told The BBC,

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.260
"I am, whether I like it or not, a public
figure during certain periods.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.920
Then I disappear, it seems."

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.960
"Of course in my experience I don't disappear,
I'm just doing other things.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.120
What I'm doing is re-engaging with life."

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.180
At this point, given his merit as an actor,
any movie starring Day-Lewis earns special

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.720
attention—which undermines his interests
in making movies in the first place.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.140
As he told The Telegraph,

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:15.280
"The work itself is never anything but pure
pleasure, but there's an awful lot of peripheral

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.360
stuff that I find it hard to be surrounded
by.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.940
I like things to be swift, because the energy
you have is concentrated and can be fleeting.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.960
The great machinery of film can work against
that."

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:30.060
In today's Hollywood, that machinery seems
larger and louder than ever—and it's left

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.440
less room for the quieter sorts of character
studies that Day-Lewis tends to favor.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:39.560
Instead, studios are looking for properties
with proven brand value, which means lots

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.680
of sequels, reboots, remakes, and cinematic
universes.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:48.020
To an actor who built his career on taking
emotional and physical risks, it may no longer

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.280
be worth working within those constraints.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.660
"Oh, oh oh, well in that case…"

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:57.940
Going out on top

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.120
When Day-Lewis won the Best Actor Oscar for
Lincoln in 2012, he made Academy Awards history

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.400
by becoming the only actor to win three trophies
in that category.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:12.440
Once you've set such a high bar for yourself,
there's really nowhere else to go but down,

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:16.510
and Day-Lewis seems to know that his status
as a legend is well worth protecting.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:18.900
"I have a competition in me.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.760
I want no one else to succeed."

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.340
And while this time seems like the real deal,
Daniel Day-Lewis has walked away from acting

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.980
before—more than once, in fact.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.460
He left a stage production of Hamlet in the
middle of a show because he thought he was

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.480
interacting with the ghost of his dead father.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.520
He took five years off after The Boxer, and
planned a five-year sabbatical from acting

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.280
after Lincoln.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.240
He told The Guardian, "I just wanted some
time away from it all.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.870
I need that quite often … I have quite a
strong feeling about when I should work and

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:50.370
when I shouldn't."

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.140
Evidently, Day-Lewis is still going with his
gut—and walking away from the spotlight

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:55.860
at last.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:57.420
Thanks for watching!

